public static void main(String[] args){
  Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
  int a = sc.nextInt();
  sc.nextLine(); // Consume the leftover newline character
  String [] str = new String[a];
  int count = 0;
  for(int i = 0 ; i<a;i++){
    str[i] = sc.nextLine();
    if(str[i].equals("Takahashi")){
     count++;  
    }


  }
  System.out.println(count);


}

fix_code_raw_response:
The issue in the provided code is that `sc.nextLine()` is used to read the strings, but after reading the integer `a` with `sc.nextInt()`, there is a newline character left in the input buffer. This causes the first call to `sc.nextLine()` to read an empty string instead of the first actual string. To fix this, we need to consume the leftover newline character after reading the integer.

Here is the patch to fix the issue:

